<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devanampiya Tissa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Tissa of Anuradhapura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devanampiya Tissa was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in the 3rd century BCE and a contemporary and ally of the Maurya emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, the monk Mahinda, Ashoka's son, converted him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and a sapling of the Bodhi tree was planted in Anuradhapura. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> monastery.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tissa was the second son of Mutasiva of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, the chief source for his reign, which in turn draws on the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipavamsa">Dipavamsa</a>, calls him foremost among his brothers in virtue and intelligence. The modified chronology adopted by modern scholars such as Wilhelm Geiger places his reign from 247 to 207 BCE. Sinhala tradition also knows him as Devanape Tis, and his title Devānaṃpiya means Beloved of the Gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, Tissa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> had long been friends without ever meeting. Tissa sent the Maurya emperor gifts, and Ashoka answered with gifts of his own, the news of his conversion and a plea that Tissa adopt the faith too. Tissa instead took the name Devānaṃpiya and had himself consecrated King of Lanka in a lavish ceremony, and he was later crowned a second time at the Royal Mandapa at Ashoka's request.</p><p>The chronicle tells that while hunting with some 40,000 soldiers near Mihintale, on the full moon day of Poson by tradition, the king chased a stag into a thicket and met the monk Mahinda, whom he at first took for a demon. Mahinda tested his wit with riddles about mango trees and kinsfolk, then preached to the king's company and presided over his conversion. At Mahinda's request Tissa asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> to send his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanghamitta">Sanghamitta</a> with other nuns and a branch of the Buddha's Bo tree, which was planted in the Mahamegha park. Tradition names his younger brothers Uttiya and Mahasiva as his successors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>With so few sources, the practical effect of the conversion on his reign is hard to judge, and none of the temples credited to him, among them a Tissamahavihara, can be reliably located. Mihintale, where he met Mahinda, is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>'s most sacred sites, with the Ambasthala or Mango tree stupa, Mahinda's cave and the Maha Seya, which holds a relic of the Buddha. The Sri Maha Bodhi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> is regarded as the oldest tree in the world planted by people. Tissa also built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tissa-wewa">Tissa Wewa</a>, a reservoir of 550 acres with an embankment 2 miles long, still a major irrigation tank for the farmers of Anuradhapura.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Devanampiya Tissa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 3rd century BCE, under <b>Devanampiya Tissa</b>, the monk Mahinda, son of the Maurya emperor Ashoka, brought Buddhism, and a sapling of the Bodhi tree was planted in the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Mahavamsa, Ruhuna was founded in the 3rd century BCE by Mahanaga, a brother of <b>Devanampiya Tissa</b> of the Anuradhapura Kingdom, who settled in the south-east of the island and built Mahagama, modern Tissamaharama.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ruhuna">Kingdom of Ruhuna</a></li></ul></section>
